   "text": {
    "L1": "Cotton comes from the fluffy fibres around the seeds of a cotton plant.",
    "L2": "A common myth says that one long fibre is pulled straight out to make thread.",
    "L3": "That is not true. Spinning twists many short fibres together into a longer, stronger yarn.",
    "L4": "The twisting can happen on a charkha or in a mill.",
    "L5": "Weaving crosses two sets of threads over and under each other on a loom to make cloth.",
    "L6": "Knitting is different: it makes cloth by looping a single long yarn, as in a sweater.",
    "L7": "For cotton cloth, pick the cotton, remove the seeds, spin the fibres into thread, and then weave."
   },
